# Retention playbook

How to run one retention wave with the recommended rule: who to contact, with which offer, through which channel, at what budget, and how to know whether it worked.

## 1. Objective and rule

**Objective:** maximise expected net retention value from a fixed budget while keeping contact fair, lawful and measurable.

**Rule:** {{ strategy_label }}. For every customer and every eligible offer:

> Expected net value (EV) = p(churn) x save rate x customer value - (offer cost + contact cost)
>
> Customer value = monthly charges x margin {{ cfg.value.margin | pct(0) }} x discounted months (base {{ cfg.value.base_horizon_months }} months x tenure factor {{ cfg.value.tenure_factors.new }}/{{ cfg.value.tenure_factors.developing }}/{{ cfg.value.tenure_factors.loyal }}, {{ cfg.value.discount_rate_annual | pct(0) }} a year)

The optimiser picks at most one offer per customer to maximise total EV within the budget, never contacts a customer with EV <= 0, and never contacts more than {{ cfg.campaign.max_contact_share | pct(0) }} of customers in a wave.

{% set w = worked_example %}
**Worked example:** a month-to-month customer paying {{ w.monthly_charges | usd }} a month with {{ w.tenure }} months of tenure (factor {{ cfg.value.tenure_factors.developing }}) is worth {{ w.monthly_charges | usd }} x {{ cfg.value.margin | pct(0) }} x {{ w.discounted_months | num(2) }} discounted months = **{{ w.value | usd }}**. With a churn probability of {{ w.p | pct(0) }} and the standard offer (save rate {{ default_offer.save_rate | pct(0) }}, cost {{ w.cost | usd }}), EV = {{ w.p }} x {{ default_offer.save_rate }} x {{ w.value | usd }} - {{ w.cost | usd }} = **{{ w.ev | usd(2) }}**. At this risk level the offer pays only for customers worth more than {{ w.min_value | usd }}.

